From a355977665f38e29522092d2675fc110fb0159f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 22:57:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] AmgX: solvers built on the C interface of AmgX

The solvers of AmgX no longer use the wrapper of MFEM, whose resources,
shared by its solvers, keep the communicator and the configuration of
the first one (destroying it made the other ones fail), on the device 0,
without access to the status of AmgX nor single precision.

- mfem-mgis uses AmgX directly (option enable-amgx, AmgX given by
  AMGX_DIR, see FindAMGX.cmake): its shared library precedes MFEM in the
  link line, a MFEM compiled with AmgX containing its own copy of AmgX.
- The resources of AmgX hold global objects (pool of memory, handles of
  cuSPARSE and cuBLAS) released when any resources are destroyed, and
  keep the address of their communicator: they are shared by all the
  solvers of the process, created on a duplicate of MPI_COMM_WORLD for
  the first solver and destroyed with the last one. The communicator of
  a parallel solver must contain all the processes.
- AmgX computes on the current device of the process; without GPU, the
  factory reports an error. The errors of AmgX are reported by its
  return codes (an invalid configuration is refused by the factory).
- The convergence of AmgXPCG is given by the status of AmgX.
- Option Precision: Double, or Single (computations of AmgX in single
  precision, the vectors being converted on the device). The mixed
  precision of AmgX is not supported by AmgX 2.5. In single precision,
  the relative tolerance of AmgXPCG must be at least 1e-6.
- The zero initial guess is uploaded (AMGX_vector_set_zero resizes the
  vector without the halo of a distributed matrix).
- Review: the verbose configuration computes the residual, the
  separators ';' and the spaces are refused in the parameters of AmgX,
  the tests take the GPU alone.
- AmgXSolverTest: destruction of the first solver, sequential solver
  created before the parallel ones, relative and absolute tolerances,
  single precision, statistics printed by AmgX.
